\chapter{Building and installing the compiler}
\label{ch:3}
\index{Building and installing the compiler}
\index{LibStone!scanner generators}

Build from the MuLIB root with a C11 and C++20 toolchain, CMake 3.20 or later, and Python 3. The LibStone gitlink pins the scanner generators. CMake can use the initialized submodule or a compatible checkout supplied with \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_LIBSTONE\_ROOT}. Configure the scanner policy explicitly if reproducing a test run. \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_REQUIRE\_LIBSTONE=ON} fails when the generators are unavailable, while \OnxCodeTerm{ON1X\_USE\_FALLBACK\_SCANNER=ON} selects the portable bootstrap scanner.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
git submodule update --init on1x/third_party/libstone
cmake -S on1x -B build/on1x -DBUILD_TESTING=ON
cmake --build build/on1x --parallel 4
ctest --test-dir build/on1x --output-on-failure
cmake --install build/on1x --prefix "$PWD/build/on1x-install"
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

A separate build directory for each profile keeps CMake caches and generated outputs distinct. Installed compiler, standard-library resources, and SDKs can be tested from the prefix without relying on a source checkout. If a command works in the build tree and fails after relocation, inspect installed resource discovery before changing the source program. Optional backend packages and analysis libraries need the additional steps in their own manuals.

\section{Identify the build you actually made}
Look for the configure message beginning ``On1x scanner:''. In the
generated configuration CMake finds two Perl scripts under the LibStone
root. It arranges for the grammar and ASDL descriptions to be processed
into C sources under the binary directory. In the fallback configuration
it compiles the checked-in bootstrap implementation instead. Both produce
an executable called \OnxCodeTerm{on1xc}; the filename cannot tell you
which scanner a token diagnostic came from. Keep the configure message
beside the diagnostic. Requiring the generators and forcing the fallback
in the same cache is a configuration error, not a way to compare them.

For an installation exercise, distinguish two things you can move. The
CMake installation includes tools that can compile a program. The standard
library source package preserves the module tree and selected native
companions for use by such tools. If handed only the latter, select it as
the standard library for an existing compiler using \OnxCodeTerm{--stdlib};
its directory is not by itself a compiler installation. A successful module
lookup answers only the source half of this test. Build and run a program
that actually uses a Basis adapter to test the native half: the build
helper must also find and compile the adapter's C source and link it with
the runtime. A failure there calls for checking the selected SDK and library
roots before investigating the module's grammar.

